<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 25</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 25 \u00b7 Cost &amp; Site</div><h1>Flooring &amp; Structural Floor Systems</h1><p>Distinguish structural framing from decorative or finished flooring systems.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Specialty structural subfloor assembly</td><td>$3.00\u2013$4.75</td><td>per SF installed, historical; 3/4-inch PT plywood is standard</td></tr><tr><td>Additional floor reinforcement</td><td>Written proposal</td><td>design/load-specific, engineer review</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section><section class=\"asi-option-mini-gallery\"><h2>Visual comparison \u2014 planned photographs</h2><p>Images will be added where they help explain actual differences. No placeholder is represented as an installed product or completed ASI project.</p><div class=\"asi-mini-grid\"><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Overall appearance</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Close-up detail</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Installed scale / context</div></div></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>Compared with a conventional \u00be-inch plywood or premium engineered-panel floor, the 2\u00d76 T&amp;G system provides a substantially thicker solid-wood deck, excellent stiffness, a traditional heavy-duty appearance and a floor that feels more substantial under equipment and foot traffic. It is priced as an upgrade above the standard floor already included in the building\u2014not as a second full floor charge.</p><p>National shed builders commonly use treated plywood or engineered tongue-and-groove panels as their primary structural floor decking. Old Hickory, for example, offers both treated plywood and engineered tongue-and-groove floor systems with thicker upgrades available for increased capacity.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>The Most-Requested Structural Floor Upgrade Is 2\u00d76 Pressure-Treated Tongue-and-Groove Southern Yellow Pine.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Compared with a conventional \u00be-inch plywood or premium engineered-panel floor, the 2\u00d76 T&amp;G system provides a substantially thicker solid-wood deck, excellent stiffness, a traditional heavy-duty appearance and a floor that feels more substantial under equipment and foot traffic. It is priced as an upgrade above the standard floor already included in the building\u2014not as a second full floor charge.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Start With the Structural Deck Before Choosing the Finish Surface</h3><p>National shed builders commonly use treated plywood or engineered tongue-and-groove panels as their primary structural floor decking. Old Hickory, for example, offers both treated plywood and engineered tongue-and-groove floor systems with thicker upgrades available for increased capacity.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>2\u00d76 T&amp;G Pressure-Treated Solid Wood</h3><p>For customers who want a more substantial structural deck than a \u00be-inch panel floor, ASI can upgrade to pressure-treated 2\u00d76 tongue-and-groove Southern Yellow Pine.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>2\u00d76 Pressure-Treated Tongue-and-Groove Structural Floor</h3><p>The upgrade table below includes premium T&amp;G material, additional installation labor, overhead / warranty / margin allowance and a credit for the standard floor that the premium floor replaces.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>The Customer Is Not Charged Twice for the Floor.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The standard \u00be-inch floor allowance is credited back before the final T&amp;G upgrade price is established.</p><p>Approximately twice the thickness of a \u00be-inch panel floor</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Top 10 Shed Flooring Options \u2014 Features, Use &amp; Cincinnati Tri-State Planning Costs</h3><p>The list below combines common national shed-builder structural floor choices with the finish surfaces most often used in workshops, garages, hobby sheds, offices and studios.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Structural Deck and Finish Flooring Are Different Layers.</h3><p>LVP, rubber, PVC tiles and LuxGuard normally install over an existing plywood or engineered structural floor. They do not replace the framing or structural subfloor unless specifically designed to do so.</p><p>Why it is popular: strong, familiar, rot-resistant and practical for storage and mower buildings.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Choose the Flooring by Building Use</h3><p>The floor should match the moisture, weight, traffic and appearance requirements of the building.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Start With the Structural Floor \u2014 Then Choose the Surface</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Appalachian Sheds can match the floor system to mower loads, workshop use, wet traffic, finished-office expectations and future flooring so the customer understands both the structure underfoot and the surface they will actually see.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Photograph the proposed building location, note approximate slope and access limitations, then request a property walk-through so the actual foundation and site scope can be documented in writing.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"references\"><h2>Relevant planning references</h2><p>These official and manufacturer resources provide additional background. Product and jurisdiction requirements must be checked for the exact project.</p><p><a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/site-excavated-final-grade-sloped-away-home\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">U.S. DOE: site grading and drainage</a></p><p><a href=\"https://bsesc.energy.gov/energy-basics/foundation-drain-gutters-downspouts\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">U.S. DOE: runoff around foundations</a></p></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">The Most-Requested Structural Floor Upgrade Is 2\u00d76 Pressure-Treated Tongue-and-Groove Southern Yellow Pine.</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">The Customer Is Not Charged Twice for the Floor.</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Start With the Structural Floor \u2014 Then Choose the Surface</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a href=\"#references\">Planning references</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

